WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.100
I want you to just look down at your arm for a second.

00:03.260 --> 00:03.420
Okay.

00:03.520 --> 00:07.820
And I want you to imagine a very mundane, but, well, a very bizarre scenario.

00:08.080 --> 00:08.320
Right.

00:08.740 --> 00:14.700
Picture this tiny, totally localized patch of migrating skin cells right on your elbow.

00:15.200 --> 00:17.420
Like maybe you just scraped it against a door frame or something.

00:17.460 --> 00:17.780
Ouch.

00:18.160 --> 00:18.340
Yeah.

00:18.480 --> 00:19.120
We've all been there.

00:19.180 --> 00:19.460
Right.

00:19.460 --> 00:24.800
And right now, millions of these microscopic cells are just, they're slowly coordinating.

00:24.980 --> 00:28.640
They're moving together to seal that gap and heal the wound.

00:28.900 --> 00:29.060
Right.

00:29.060 --> 00:32.740
Now take that mental image, you know, of your skin cells doing their thing.

00:32.840 --> 00:36.060
And I want you to hold it up next to a completely different biological process.

00:36.740 --> 00:36.960
Okay.

00:37.080 --> 00:37.440
What is it?

00:37.540 --> 00:42.520
The structural brain development of a human being who has just been diagnosed with generalized anxiety disorder.

00:42.700 --> 00:43.080
Oh, wow.

00:43.340 --> 00:43.540
Right.

00:43.760 --> 00:47.280
We are talking about two wildly disparate phenomena here.

00:47.400 --> 00:50.100
I mean, one is this microscopic tissue repair.

00:50.240 --> 00:50.380
Yeah.

00:50.500 --> 00:52.040
Unfolding over just a few hours.

00:52.180 --> 00:52.620
Exactly.

00:52.720 --> 00:54.420
In a very specific physical space.

00:54.420 --> 01:00.880
And the other is this massively complex psychiatric neurodevelopmental trajectory.

01:01.420 --> 01:05.340
And that one is unfolding invisibly over like decades.

01:05.720 --> 01:06.880
To a completely different scale.

01:07.000 --> 01:07.280
Totally.

01:07.540 --> 01:15.440
And if you asked, you know, a doctor or a biologist or a psychiatrist what those two things have in common, they would probably look at you like you were crazy.

01:15.440 --> 01:16.320
Oh, absolutely.

01:16.860 --> 01:18.860
They would tell you they have absolutely nothing in common.

01:18.980 --> 01:26.500
I mean, if you look at them through the, well, the traditional orthodox lens of biology, they are just entirely separate disciplines.

01:26.680 --> 01:26.780
Right.

01:26.940 --> 01:27.440
Completely siloed.

01:27.440 --> 01:28.440
Yeah.

01:28.440 --> 01:30.840
They're studied by different scientists.

01:31.060 --> 01:35.220
They're sitting in totally different university buildings, publishing in different journals.

01:35.560 --> 01:37.220
Using completely separate vocabularies.

01:37.220 --> 01:37.620
Exactly.

01:37.800 --> 01:41.140
They use completely different words to even describe what they see.

01:41.340 --> 01:44.400
But what if they aren't so different?

01:45.200 --> 01:54.540
What if the underlying mathematical geometry like the invisible architectural rules dictating how your skin cells move to close that wound?

01:55.000 --> 01:55.180
Yeah.

01:55.180 --> 02:00.180
And how an anxious brain physically wires itself over a whole lifetime.

02:00.940 --> 02:03.860
What if they are actually governed by the exact same equation?

02:04.060 --> 02:09.460
I mean, that is the very bold, highly counterintuitive claim that we're unpacking today.

02:09.740 --> 02:11.700
Welcome to today's deep dive.

02:11.900 --> 02:12.640
It's a fun one.

02:12.920 --> 02:14.040
It really is.

02:14.400 --> 02:19.140
Our mission for this session is to explore this truly fascinating theoretical paper.

02:19.340 --> 02:21.860
It's by an independent researcher named Fliction.

02:22.120 --> 02:24.840
And it's a dense paper, but so rewarding.

02:24.840 --> 02:26.500
Oh, incredibly dense.

02:26.880 --> 02:33.680
It's titled, Toward a Field Theoretic Interpretation of Neural Heterogeneity and Collective Cell Migration.

02:33.920 --> 02:34.640
Quite a mouthful.

02:34.800 --> 02:35.800
Yeah, it really is.

02:36.080 --> 02:40.580
But basically, it takes two recent cutting-edge empirical studies.

02:40.580 --> 02:45.160
One is in translational psychiatry, looking at how the anxious brain ages.

02:45.380 --> 02:45.540
Right.

02:45.700 --> 02:49.780
And the other is in cell biology, looking at how tissue migrates.

02:50.200 --> 02:51.160
And it unites them.

02:51.260 --> 02:51.600
It does.

02:51.680 --> 02:54.760
It weaves them together using this unified mathematical framework.

02:54.840 --> 02:57.620
It's called the Relativistic Scalar Vector Plenum.

02:57.620 --> 02:59.960
Or the RSVP formalism, for short.

03:00.120 --> 03:01.160
And goodness for acronyms.

03:01.240 --> 03:01.620
Seriously.

03:02.040 --> 03:07.580
But to really grasp what this paper is doing, we have to be willing to abandon a very comfortable,

03:07.920 --> 03:10.800
very deeply ingrained way of thinking about life.

03:10.880 --> 03:11.240
We do.

03:11.400 --> 03:14.660
Because usually, we just think of biology as a collection of static parts.

03:15.120 --> 03:15.280
Right.

03:15.280 --> 03:16.020
Like a machine.

03:16.380 --> 03:16.780
Exactly.

03:17.480 --> 03:20.880
A cell is a little microscopic machine with protein gears.

03:21.280 --> 03:25.220
And a brain is just a bigger machine with neural wires.

03:25.420 --> 03:26.360
And if something breaks.

03:26.540 --> 03:26.740
Right.

03:26.800 --> 03:31.200
If a person gets sick or a tissue fails, you just, you look for the broken gear.

03:31.520 --> 03:34.780
Or you look for the frayed wire and you try to fix it.

03:34.800 --> 03:35.820
You swap out the part.

03:36.000 --> 03:36.220
Yeah.

03:36.480 --> 03:40.540
But this paper asks you to just stop looking at the parts entirely.

03:40.760 --> 03:41.580
Which is hard to do.

03:41.920 --> 03:42.520
It's very hard.

03:42.520 --> 03:48.480
But instead, it asks you to view biology as this fluid, dynamic dance of what the author

03:48.480 --> 03:50.540
calls constraint redistribution.

03:51.040 --> 03:52.300
Constrain redistribution.

03:52.380 --> 03:52.600
Yeah.

03:52.800 --> 03:54.540
It's not about the physical stuff itself.

03:54.740 --> 03:59.480
It's about the invisible space of possibilities available to that stuff at any given millisecond.

03:59.620 --> 03:59.800
Okay.

03:59.860 --> 04:00.760
Let's unpack this.

04:00.940 --> 04:04.520
Because abandoning the idea of parts, like you said, is incredibly hard.

04:04.740 --> 04:06.000
It's how we're trained to think.

04:06.280 --> 04:06.660
Literally.

04:06.920 --> 04:09.580
It is literally how we have done science since the Enlightenment.

04:09.580 --> 04:14.320
So, before we get into the brain age study or the migrating skin cells, we need to spend

04:14.320 --> 04:15.240
some time on this.

04:15.580 --> 04:18.980
We need to talk about why this old way of thinking is falling short.

04:19.360 --> 04:19.660
Right.

04:19.760 --> 04:22.380
What the paper calls the entity-centered trap.

04:22.840 --> 04:25.600
Why is it suddenly failing in modern laboratories?

04:25.940 --> 04:29.680
Well, the paper refers to this traditional approach as being strongly localist.

04:30.660 --> 04:32.480
And, you know, I want to be clear here.

04:33.100 --> 04:34.800
We shouldn't dismiss it entirely.

04:34.960 --> 04:36.240
Because it works sometimes, right?

04:36.240 --> 04:39.600
Oh, it has been phenomenally successful in certain domains.

04:39.740 --> 04:41.760
If you're looking at classical Mendelian genetics.

04:41.960 --> 04:43.000
The funnets squares and all that.

04:43.140 --> 04:43.640
Exactly.

04:43.800 --> 04:45.280
Or basic structural biochemistry.

04:45.940 --> 04:49.460
This entity-centered framework is fantastic.

04:49.860 --> 04:50.920
You find a gene.

04:51.520 --> 04:54.560
You find the exact sequence of amino acids it codes for.

04:54.600 --> 04:54.840
Right.

04:55.140 --> 04:57.740
You determine the three-dimensional shape of that resulting protein.

04:57.920 --> 04:59.460
And, boom, you understand the disease.

04:59.680 --> 05:00.760
Like sickle cell anemia.

05:00.960 --> 05:01.800
Perfect example.

05:01.800 --> 05:09.700
One genetic mutation changes one amino acid in the hemoglobin protein that changes its shape, making the red blood cells sickle.

05:10.000 --> 05:13.760
It's a beautifully linear mechanistic chain of cause and effect.

05:13.880 --> 05:14.520
It really is.

05:14.940 --> 05:24.280
The strongly localist view treats macroscopic phenomena, you know, the big things we observe, as just the aggregated result of discrete individual components.

05:24.600 --> 05:28.980
Just parts interacting based on their fixed intrinsic properties.

05:29.320 --> 05:29.660
Yes.

05:29.660 --> 05:31.700
I always go back to the car analogy for this.

05:31.960 --> 05:36.840
You know, a car is made of a steering wheel, an engine block, four tires, and brake pads.

05:37.100 --> 05:37.320
Right.

05:37.520 --> 05:43.480
If you want to understand how the car works or, like, why it broke down, you take it apart.

05:43.660 --> 05:44.600
You pull the engine out.

05:44.680 --> 05:44.900
Right.

05:45.160 --> 05:48.100
You study the engine in complete isolation on a workbench.

05:48.280 --> 05:50.000
You study the tires in isolation.

05:50.460 --> 05:54.960
And then you just add all those isolated understandings up and you have a complete understanding of the vehicle.

05:55.280 --> 05:57.080
And that works perfectly for a car.

05:57.080 --> 06:01.400
But here is where the localist framework just falls flat in biology.

06:01.900 --> 06:02.660
Where does it break down?

06:03.660 --> 06:11.780
It fails spectacularly when you are trying to understand coherent, large-scale behaviors that emerge from distributed interactions.

06:11.860 --> 06:12.000
Okay.

06:12.020 --> 06:13.060
Give me an example of that.

06:13.180 --> 06:19.320
Like when the behavior of the whole biological system isn't dictated by the intrinsic properties of any single component.

06:19.580 --> 06:23.460
It's dictated by the dynamic, fleeting relationships between them.

06:23.640 --> 06:24.040
Ah.

06:24.040 --> 06:26.240
So you can't just isolate one piece.

06:26.560 --> 06:26.960
Exactly.

06:27.420 --> 06:32.280
In those complex cases, looking for the broken part is just a fool's errand.

06:32.500 --> 06:34.460
You are never going to find it sitting on the workbench.

06:34.680 --> 06:39.400
I think of it like trying to understand a stadium wave at a massive sports game.

06:39.560 --> 06:40.680
Oh, I love this analogy.

06:40.940 --> 06:41.200
Right.

06:41.200 --> 06:49.260
Imagine you are an alien biologist, you just landed on Earth, and you are trying to understand what a stadium wave is and how it functions.

06:49.740 --> 06:50.060
Okay.

06:50.220 --> 06:51.720
The alien has his clipboard.

06:51.820 --> 06:52.060
Right.

06:52.360 --> 07:00.100
If you take that strongly localist, entity-centered approach, you'd go into the stadium, capture a single sports fan.

07:00.360 --> 07:01.500
Beam them up to the spaceship.

07:01.500 --> 07:02.180
Exactly.

07:02.540 --> 07:08.620
Take them back to your spacious laboratory and study the physical properties of their kneecap or their elbow joint.

07:08.740 --> 07:08.880
Right.

07:08.960 --> 07:09.820
You'd measure everything.

07:10.140 --> 07:14.840
You could measure their muscle mass, their reaction time, their blood pressure, their caloric intake.

07:15.200 --> 07:20.040
You could have the most detailed, high-resolution data on that individual human being.

07:20.040 --> 07:23.740
And you would never, ever find the stadium wave inside them.

07:23.880 --> 07:24.860
Not a chance.

07:25.000 --> 07:27.400
That perfectly captures the limitation.

07:27.660 --> 07:27.800
Yeah.

07:27.980 --> 07:31.680
You miss the overarching, invisible dynamic completely.

07:32.220 --> 07:34.300
The wave is not a property of the fan.

07:34.760 --> 07:36.400
No, it's a property of the crowd.

07:36.700 --> 07:45.000
It is a property of the coupled medium, the crowd, and how a constraint or a signal propagates through that medium over time and space.

07:45.180 --> 07:47.020
The fan is just the substrate.

07:47.260 --> 07:49.080
The phenomenon is the wave.

07:49.240 --> 07:49.720
Precisely.

07:49.720 --> 07:57.780
So basically, biology has been obsessively studying millions of kneecaps for decades, wondering why they can't figure out the stadium wave.

07:58.160 --> 08:00.060
That is a very accurate way to put it, yeah.

08:00.220 --> 08:07.980
And to prove exactly how this localist view fails in practice, Fliction points to the first of our two empirical studies in the source material.

08:08.040 --> 08:09.000
Right, the brain study.

08:09.140 --> 08:09.360
Yeah.

08:09.640 --> 08:15.600
This is where we dive into the geometry of anxiety and specifically the mystery of the aging brain.

08:15.600 --> 08:19.040
So this brings us to a major study by Richie Ayer and collaborators.

08:19.040 --> 08:22.740
It was conducted within the Enigma Anxiety Consortium.

08:22.780 --> 08:24.700
Which is a massive global initiative.

08:24.940 --> 08:25.420
Huge.

08:25.660 --> 08:26.780
Data from all over the world.

08:26.900 --> 08:29.980
They were investigating generalized anxiety disorder or GAD.

08:30.300 --> 08:30.480
Okay.

08:30.480 --> 08:34.080
And their core question was actually quite straightforward, they asked.

08:34.220 --> 08:38.080
Is having GAD associated with accelerated structural brain aging?

08:38.380 --> 08:42.740
Which, I mean, that makes intuitive sense to anyone who has ever experienced severe stress.

08:42.940 --> 08:43.480
Oh, for sure.

08:43.480 --> 08:49.060
You know, chronic anxiety, elevated cortisol levels, just flooding the system all the time.

08:49.120 --> 08:50.940
The constant state of hyperarousal.

08:51.080 --> 08:51.280
Right.

08:51.720 --> 08:56.140
You would naturally assume that this wears the physical structure of the brain down faster.

08:56.480 --> 08:59.120
It acts like an accelerant on the aging process.

08:59.340 --> 09:02.000
And that was the implicit assumption underlying the research, yes.

09:02.000 --> 09:10.540
The traditional disease model reasoning always predicts that pathology acts as a directional force.

09:10.740 --> 09:12.120
It pushes you somewhere specific.

09:12.440 --> 09:12.800
Exactly.

09:13.020 --> 09:17.240
It pushes a population along a single predictable developmental axis.

09:17.500 --> 09:18.680
So how did they test that?

09:19.240 --> 09:24.920
To test this hypothesis, the researchers used a convolutional neural network, a CNN,

09:24.920 --> 09:30.240
to estimate a metric they call the predicted age difference, or PA.

09:30.720 --> 09:33.620
Okay, let's break down how this actually works for you listening.

09:33.820 --> 09:37.520
Because the methodology here is crucial to understanding the big twist later.

09:37.680 --> 09:38.260
It really is.

09:38.260 --> 09:41.780
They didn't just have a doctor look at an MRI and like, take a guess.

09:42.360 --> 09:44.820
They trained a deep learning model, the CNN,

09:45.240 --> 09:50.360
on thousands and thousands of structural brain scans of completely healthy people.

09:50.560 --> 09:52.220
So the AI is learning the baseline.

09:52.520 --> 09:52.820
Right.

09:52.820 --> 09:58.880
The AI learns the incredibly subtle, complex patterns of what a typical 20-year-old brain looks like,

09:59.380 --> 10:03.040
and typical 40-year-old brain, a 60-year-old brain, and so on.

10:03.120 --> 10:04.660
It gets very good at guessing age.

10:04.960 --> 10:05.440
Exactly.

10:05.800 --> 10:05.960
Mm.

10:06.320 --> 10:11.500
Then you feed it a brand new scan, the brain of someone with generalized anxiety disorder,

10:11.700 --> 10:16.340
and you essentially ask the AI, based on everything you know about normal aging,

10:16.740 --> 10:18.880
how old does this physical structure look?

10:18.900 --> 10:19.080
Right.

10:19.420 --> 10:21.580
And the output of that question gives you the P8.

10:21.580 --> 10:23.280
The predicted age difference.

10:23.460 --> 10:23.700
Yes.

10:24.080 --> 10:29.520
It is the estimated brain age calculated by the algorithm minus the person's actual chronological age.

10:29.560 --> 10:29.700
Okay.

10:29.760 --> 10:31.720
So give me an example of how the math works there.

10:31.880 --> 10:34.060
Well, if your chronological age is 30,

10:34.700 --> 10:40.120
but the CNN looks at the complex folding and gray matter density of your cortex and says your brain looks 35.

10:40.520 --> 10:42.400
Then your PAD is a positive 5.

10:42.400 --> 10:42.960
Exactly.

10:43.440 --> 10:47.120
That positive value indicates a neurologically older-appearing structure.

10:47.220 --> 10:47.420
Yeah.

10:47.540 --> 10:48.840
It suggests premature aging.

10:48.880 --> 10:49.500
And the reverse.

10:49.900 --> 10:55.200
Conversely, if the CNN says your brain looks 25, your PAD is negative 5.

10:55.760 --> 11:00.220
That indicates a delayed or atypical youthfulness in the structural development.

11:00.440 --> 11:00.680
Okay.

11:00.800 --> 11:03.360
So the researchers fire up this massive analysis.

11:03.740 --> 11:09.520
They run all the data, assuming they're going to find a consistently positive PAD in the GAD patients.

11:09.520 --> 11:14.360
They fully expect the anxiety group's average to be shifted firmly towards premature aging.

11:14.680 --> 11:16.940
Proving that chronic stress wears the brain out.

11:17.040 --> 11:17.240
Right.

11:17.520 --> 11:21.460
But then the data comes back and we get the twist.

11:21.560 --> 11:21.960
The twist.

11:22.140 --> 11:22.600
What happened?

11:22.680 --> 11:27.080
The central result completely contradicted the original intuitive hypothesis.

11:27.460 --> 11:28.640
The mean didn't shift.

11:28.800 --> 11:29.060
Nope.

11:29.060 --> 11:35.580
The individuals with GAD did not show a significantly elevated mean PAD compared to the healthy control group.

11:36.260 --> 11:41.720
If you just looked at the statistical averages, the anxious brains looked exactly the same age as the healthy non-anxious brains.

11:41.860 --> 11:42.040
Okay.

11:42.160 --> 11:42.440
Hold on.

11:42.500 --> 11:43.660
I have to play devil's advocate here.

11:43.800 --> 11:44.320
Go for it.

11:44.460 --> 11:48.840
If the average is exactly the same, how do we know the AI isn't just failing?

11:49.220 --> 11:51.380
Or how do we know this isn't just noise in the scans?

11:51.380 --> 11:58.460
I mean, if I'm a traditional entity-centered researcher, I look at that zero mean shift and say, well, the study failed.

11:58.900 --> 11:59.520
Nothing to see here.

11:59.760 --> 11:59.940
Right.

12:00.320 --> 12:01.860
GAD doesn't affect brain aging.

12:02.080 --> 12:03.960
There is no biological signal here.

12:04.040 --> 12:05.360
Let's move on to the next disease.

12:05.660 --> 12:11.360
And that reaction is exactly the trap of the entity-centered mindset.

12:11.580 --> 12:11.940
Uh-huh.

12:12.040 --> 12:15.460
Because there was a massive, profound biological signal in the data.

12:15.720 --> 12:17.600
It just wasn't a shift in the mean.

12:17.680 --> 12:18.300
What was it?

12:18.300 --> 12:26.360
What the researchers found, and this was particularly in the adults older than 25, was an absolute explosion in the variance of the PAD.

12:26.560 --> 12:28.980
The variance, so the spread of the data point.

12:29.020 --> 12:29.980
Exactly, the spread.

12:30.500 --> 12:32.680
The mean stayed near zero, right in the middle.

12:32.860 --> 12:33.000
Right.

12:33.020 --> 12:35.920
But the data points scattered wildly in both directions.

12:36.000 --> 12:37.960
So they weren't clustered around zero at all?

12:38.160 --> 12:38.660
Not at all.

12:38.660 --> 12:43.460
Some individuals with GAD displayed neural profiles consistent with highly accelerated aging.

12:43.780 --> 12:45.480
They had very high positive PAs.

12:45.620 --> 12:47.580
So their brains looked decades older.

12:47.580 --> 12:48.540
Decades.

12:49.040 --> 12:57.940
But other individuals with the exact same diagnosis displayed profiles consistent with severely delayed or highly plastic atypical development.

12:58.020 --> 13:00.080
They had very high negative PAs.

13:00.260 --> 13:00.460
Right.

13:01.040 --> 13:04.820
Their brains looked radically underdeveloped or artificially youthful.

13:04.900 --> 13:06.680
Man, this is unsettling to think about.

13:07.120 --> 13:07.520
It is.

13:07.520 --> 13:17.000
It means the disorder didn't push everyone in one uniform pathological direction, like the diagnostic category of generalized anxiety disorder in the DSM.

13:17.160 --> 13:21.240
It doesn't map to a single broken state in the physical brain.

13:21.520 --> 13:21.720
No.

13:21.720 --> 13:25.360
It maps to this broadened, heterogeneous cloud of possibilities.

13:25.640 --> 13:28.800
Ampliction calls this phenomenon trajectory space dilation.

13:29.240 --> 13:30.200
Trajectory space dilation.

13:30.840 --> 13:32.360
Okay, how should we visualize that?

13:32.360 --> 13:38.680
Think of the space of all possible structural brain configurations as a vast landscape, like a topographical map.

13:38.720 --> 13:39.540
Okay, I picture it.

13:39.680 --> 13:46.240
A healthy, typical developmental trajectory traces a relatively predictable narrow path through that landscape.

13:46.360 --> 13:47.400
A well-worn trail.

13:47.400 --> 13:48.080
Exactly.

13:48.480 --> 13:51.880
As you age, your neural architecture consolidates.

13:52.500 --> 14:00.340
It prunes away unused connections, it myelinates the important ones, and eventually settles into a specific, stable adult configuration.

14:00.680 --> 14:03.240
You find your groove on the map, and you just stay in it.

14:03.420 --> 14:03.700
Right.

14:04.420 --> 14:07.720
But the GAD population is spread out all over this landscape.

14:08.160 --> 14:09.100
They aren't in a groove.

14:09.300 --> 14:10.460
They are completely scattered.

14:10.720 --> 14:11.440
Totally scattered.

14:11.900 --> 14:15.800
And Fliction argues that this is the defining feature of the pathology.

14:15.800 --> 14:22.480
GAD doesn't primarily operate by shifting the brain's developmental trajectory in a single direction.

14:22.640 --> 14:25.260
Which is what mathematics would call a simple translation.

14:25.480 --> 14:26.180
Right, a translation.

14:26.640 --> 14:28.900
Instead, it acts as a dilation.

14:29.420 --> 14:33.260
It massively expands the space of accessible neural trajectories.

14:33.360 --> 14:37.800
Okay, so breaking that down, some GAD brains consolidate their neural architecture prematurely.

14:38.460 --> 14:39.400
They lock down early.

14:39.480 --> 14:44.860
Yeah, you can imagine this as a rigid, structural, defensive response to chronic threat anticipation.

14:44.860 --> 14:48.060
The brain says, the world is incredibly dangerous.

14:48.440 --> 14:51.580
I need to hardwire my survival circuits right now and stop changing.

14:51.980 --> 14:52.240
Wow.

14:52.700 --> 14:54.840
But others go the complete opposite route.

14:55.140 --> 15:00.180
They remain in these highly plastic, diffuse, delayed states.

15:00.320 --> 15:04.220
Because for them, the brain says, the world is entirely unpredictable.

15:04.960 --> 15:08.240
I need to stay completely malleable so I can react to anything.

15:08.240 --> 15:11.840
So the pathology isn't the specific destination they ended up in.

15:12.060 --> 15:12.220
No.

15:12.560 --> 15:18.260
The pathology is the loss of the typical, constrained developmental pathway itself.

15:18.420 --> 15:20.720
It is the loss of the biological guardrails.

15:20.860 --> 15:21.160
Yes.

15:21.320 --> 15:26.340
I really want to linger on that because that is a profound shift in how we think about mental health.

15:27.040 --> 15:28.040
You lose the guardrail.

15:28.120 --> 15:28.980
It changes everything.

15:28.980 --> 15:31.820
It's not that the developmental train went to the wrong station.

15:32.180 --> 15:39.120
It's that the train can now wander off the tracks entirely and just drift into the wilderness in multiple, completely contradictory directions.

15:39.400 --> 15:40.520
That's a great way to picture it.

15:40.660 --> 15:46.200
So what does this actually mean for a real person sitting in a psychiatrist's office?

15:46.620 --> 15:52.760
It means the disorder heavily amplifies the sensitivity of the physical brain to individual life histories.

15:52.920 --> 15:53.580
Okay, how so?

15:53.580 --> 15:58.140
Well, it makes the individual's developmental trajectory highly volatile.

15:58.740 --> 16:06.200
It's much more easily pushed toward either extreme rigidity or hyperplasticity, depending on a whole host of deeply personal factors.

16:06.280 --> 16:06.780
Like what?

16:07.240 --> 16:12.820
The severity of their symptoms, the specific medications they take, their childhood traumas, their daily environment.

16:12.940 --> 16:14.560
Diet, sleep, all of that.

16:14.900 --> 16:15.340
Exactly.

16:15.340 --> 16:23.700
All of these things suddenly have a vastly exaggerated impact on the physical wiring of the brain because those normal healthy constraints have been dissolved.

16:24.340 --> 16:27.060
But wait, why did it take a complex AI to see this?

16:27.300 --> 16:37.340
You mentioned earlier that the researchers used a convolutional neural network, but we've been taking fMRI scans and structural MRI scans of anxious brains for decades.

16:38.300 --> 16:38.980
Decades, yes.

16:39.140 --> 16:42.140
Why is this variance explosion only being noticed right now?

16:42.140 --> 16:49.320
There is a crucial methodological reason for this, and Fliction highlights it, to show exactly why the old paradigm fails.

16:49.460 --> 16:50.420
Okay, lay it on me.

16:50.800 --> 16:55.920
The Inojima researchers caught this variance explosion specifically because they used a multivariate CNN.

16:56.440 --> 16:59.000
Multivariate, meaning looking at many variables at once.

16:59.180 --> 16:59.420
Right.

16:59.820 --> 17:08.140
If they had used traditional univariate brain analysis, which has been the absolute gold standard in neuroscience for a very long time...

17:08.140 --> 17:08.960
They would have missed it.

17:09.140 --> 17:10.360
They would have completely missed it.

17:10.360 --> 17:12.440
It would have looked like pure statistical noise.

17:12.580 --> 17:13.620
Break that down for me.

17:13.620 --> 17:18.880
What is a univariate analysis doing that a CNN isn't, or vice versa?

17:19.080 --> 17:21.400
How do you miss something this massive?

17:22.420 --> 17:22.560
Okay.

17:23.000 --> 17:33.280
Traditional univariate analyses project the incredibly complex, high-dimensional reality of the brain down to a collection of isolated, individual scalar measurements.

17:33.400 --> 17:33.620
Okay.

17:33.620 --> 17:36.880
They use a technique called voxel-based morphometry.

17:37.340 --> 17:41.060
They divide the brain image into tiny 3D pixels called voxels.

17:41.200 --> 17:42.360
Little cubes of brain.

17:42.600 --> 17:42.860
Right.

17:43.580 --> 17:47.780
Then they look at voxel A and measure the gray matter density.

17:48.300 --> 17:50.780
Then they look at voxel B and measure it.

17:51.060 --> 17:52.100
Then voxel C.

17:52.260 --> 17:55.520
And they compare those individual voxels across the patient groups.

17:55.520 --> 17:55.880
Yes.

17:56.280 --> 17:57.760
Completely independently of one another.

17:57.860 --> 17:58.520
Ah, I see.

17:58.660 --> 18:08.520
It's like taking a beautiful, high-resolution digital photograph of a human face, and instead of looking at the holistic image of the face, you just write down the brightness value of the first pixel.

18:08.820 --> 18:09.680
Then the second pixel.

18:09.800 --> 18:10.420
Then the third.

18:10.420 --> 18:13.640
You completely destroy the spatial relationships.

18:13.980 --> 18:15.560
You lose the distance between the eyes.

18:15.640 --> 18:16.620
The curve of the jaw.

18:16.820 --> 18:18.840
All the stuff that actually makes up the image.

18:18.940 --> 18:20.580
That is exactly what happens.

18:20.860 --> 18:20.960
Yeah.

18:21.000 --> 18:31.980
The univariate approach preserves the local information like how thick the cortex is in one microscopic spot, but it systematically destroys the distributed relational structure.

18:32.200 --> 18:33.660
And the CNN doesn't do that.

18:33.760 --> 18:34.040
No.

18:34.300 --> 18:37.100
A convolutional neural network doesn't look at isolated pixels.

18:37.100 --> 18:42.840
It uses layers of mathematical filters called kernels that slide across the whole image.

18:43.140 --> 18:43.780
Looking for patterns.

18:44.040 --> 18:49.400
Looking for patterns, edges, gradients, and most importantly, relationships between adjacent areas.

18:50.040 --> 18:57.880
By its very mathematical nature, a CNN learns spatially distributed nonlinear functions of the full three-dimensional input.

18:58.040 --> 18:59.800
It retains the relational geometry.

19:00.020 --> 19:00.240
Yes.

19:00.240 --> 19:09.740
So Fliction is arguing that the actual physical signature of anxiety isn't located in the amygdala or the prefrontal cortex or literally any one specific brain region.

19:10.100 --> 19:10.600
Precisely.

19:11.200 --> 19:16.280
The organizational signal of GAD isn't located in any individual measurement site.

19:16.980 --> 19:23.220
It is a property of the global relational geometry of the brain's entire developmental trajectory.

19:23.560 --> 19:23.920
Wow.

19:23.920 --> 19:33.780
If you collapse the data down to independent measurements to try and find the broken part, you destroy the very information that makes this variance detectable in the first place.

19:34.180 --> 19:37.660
So in this framework, the variance isn't noise obscuring the signal?

19:37.880 --> 19:38.060
No.

19:38.180 --> 19:39.480
The variance is the signal.

19:39.720 --> 19:40.100
Exactly.

19:40.300 --> 19:40.460
Okay.

19:40.520 --> 19:52.480
So we have this deeply compelling picture of the anxious brain where the biological possibilities are just spreading out over decades of time, dilating in variance because the guardrails of typical development have been removed.

19:52.640 --> 19:52.820
Right.

19:52.820 --> 20:02.540
Now, if an anxious brain is an example of possibilities spreading out over decades, does biology ever use the same guardrail-removing trick over physical space?

20:02.700 --> 20:04.320
That is the million-dollar question.

20:04.500 --> 20:06.580
Like, in a matter of hours rather than years.

20:06.740 --> 20:13.400
Because if you look closely at how human skin heals a wound, Fliction argues we are looking at the exact same geometry.

20:13.940 --> 20:14.260
Yes.

20:14.820 --> 20:18.260
And this brings us to the second empirical study analyzed in the paper.

20:18.740 --> 20:21.320
This one was conducted by Hirano and their collaborators.

20:21.320 --> 20:23.260
And they weren't looking at brains at all.

20:23.480 --> 20:24.100
Not at all.

20:24.200 --> 20:26.500
They were looking at epithelial cell migration.

20:27.220 --> 20:28.020
Epithelial cells.

20:28.320 --> 20:31.920
So we are talking about a sheet of cells, like the outer layer of your skin.

20:31.960 --> 20:35.040
Or the protective lining of your intestines or your lungs.

20:35.040 --> 20:35.440
Yeah.

20:35.720 --> 20:46.100
And when you get a physical wound, a scrape, a cut, these epithelial cells have to migrate collectively across the physical space to close the gap and restore the barrier.

20:46.640 --> 20:46.940
Right.

20:47.220 --> 20:54.120
And, you know, the old view of how this healing process worked was, again, very entity-centered and localist.

20:54.220 --> 20:55.900
Just focusing on the individual cells.

20:55.900 --> 20:56.220
Yeah.

20:56.460 --> 21:00.660
We used to think that cells were basically semi-autonomous, independent little agents.

21:01.020 --> 21:08.280
You get a cut on your arm, and the cells immediately bordering the cut individually sense a change in the chemical environment.

21:08.400 --> 21:10.260
A process called chemotaxis, right?

21:10.320 --> 21:10.600
Exactly.

21:10.720 --> 21:11.820
They sense a chemical gradient.

21:12.040 --> 21:13.600
They independently decide to start moving.

21:14.080 --> 21:18.300
And they crawl toward the highest concentration of the chemical at the center of the wound.

21:18.300 --> 21:25.200
So the collective movement of the tissue was just seen as a statistical aggregate of millions of individual decisions.

21:25.660 --> 21:33.160
Like a huge crowd of commuters on a highway, all independently deciding to drive toward the city center, because they all individually want to get to work.

21:33.240 --> 21:33.520
Right.

21:33.780 --> 21:40.460
But that model totally falls apart when you look under a high-powered microscope at highly coordinated collective migration.

21:40.740 --> 21:41.060
It does.

21:41.060 --> 21:49.000
Because what the Hirano team discovered, using advanced live cell imaging, is that these cells do not decide independently at all.

21:49.100 --> 21:49.860
Not even close.

21:50.180 --> 21:54.820
Instead, the migration is driven by something called ERK activation raves.

21:54.980 --> 21:55.940
Let's explain what that means.

21:56.280 --> 22:00.000
ERK stands for extracellular signal-regulated kinase.

22:00.220 --> 22:01.680
Say that three times fast.

22:01.880 --> 22:02.120
Right.

22:02.420 --> 22:05.020
It is a critical signaling molecule inside the cell.

22:05.440 --> 22:10.260
But what Hirano saw was that ERK doesn't just turn on in one cell at the edge of the wound.

22:10.260 --> 22:11.700
It's not just a localized alarm.

22:11.940 --> 22:12.180
No.

22:12.380 --> 22:12.560
Yeah.

22:12.880 --> 22:22.220
The activation propagates across the entire intact epithelial sheet as a highly structured traveling wave, exactly like a ripple spreading outward in a pond.

22:22.340 --> 22:23.040
A stadium wave.

22:23.200 --> 22:23.520
Yes.

22:24.060 --> 22:27.440
The chemical signal is physically sweeping through the crowd of cells.

22:27.700 --> 22:30.340
It is a stadium wave of biochemical activation.

22:30.480 --> 22:30.840
It is.

22:31.220 --> 22:38.340
And as this wave washes over the cells, it triggers a massive, highly coordinated structural reorganization within the tissue.

22:38.340 --> 22:38.720
Okay.

22:38.840 --> 22:43.420
To understand the sheer mechanics of this reorganization, we have to talk about a specific protein.

22:43.580 --> 22:44.620
It's called ZO1.

22:44.840 --> 22:45.620
ZO1, yes.

22:45.780 --> 22:49.780
And I want to dive into the biochemistry here because this is where the story gets really visceral.

22:49.900 --> 22:51.340
It's fascinating stuff.

22:51.460 --> 22:57.760
In a normal resting layer of skin cells, ZO1 acts like a vital scaffolding protein.

22:58.300 --> 23:03.720
It sits way up at the top edges of the cells in what are called the apical junctional complexes.

23:04.060 --> 23:04.280
Right.

23:04.280 --> 23:06.560
And it maintains the tight junctions.

23:06.920 --> 23:11.580
And the tight junctions are essentially the biological glue that keeps the tissue barrier intact.

23:11.800 --> 23:17.680
They bind the membranes of adjacent cells so tightly together that fluids and pathogens can't leak through.

23:17.920 --> 23:21.760
So ZO1 is the structural linchpin keeping that barrier strong.

23:21.820 --> 23:22.180
Exactly.

23:22.340 --> 23:26.200
If you lose ZO1, your skin literally loses its integrity.

23:26.460 --> 23:27.020
It falls apart.

23:27.020 --> 23:30.180
So ZO1 is up there holding the cells together.

23:30.780 --> 23:39.420
But when that ERK activation wave rolls through the tissue, this whole biochemical cascade happens, how does the wave actually affect the protein?

23:39.800 --> 23:39.980
Okay.

23:40.060 --> 23:47.560
So when the ERK wave washes over the cell, the kinase enzyme physically phosphorylates the ZO1 protein.

23:47.940 --> 23:50.140
Phosphorylates, meaning it adds something to it.

23:50.140 --> 23:50.440
Right.

23:50.520 --> 23:56.000
It essentially attaches a bulky chemical tag of phosphate group to the protein.

23:56.400 --> 24:02.140
And this chemical tag forces the ZO1 protein to change its three-dimensional physical shape.

24:02.360 --> 24:04.140
Like a key getting bent out of shape.

24:04.220 --> 24:04.440
Yeah.

24:04.780 --> 24:08.940
Because its shape has changed, it can no longer fit into the lock of the tight junction.

24:09.160 --> 24:11.260
So it physically detaches from the membrane.

24:11.400 --> 24:12.820
And it doesn't just float away, right?

24:12.820 --> 24:19.220
It shuttles all the way down from the top of the cell to the very bottom of the cell to these specialized structures called basal protosomes.

24:19.380 --> 24:19.660
That's right.

24:20.100 --> 24:25.120
I like to picture the epithelial cells as a dense, modern city of skyscrapers.

24:25.320 --> 24:27.820
They're all linked together by high-altitude skybridges.

24:28.600 --> 24:28.740
Okay.

24:28.780 --> 24:29.280
I like this.

24:29.640 --> 24:37.000
And the ZO1 proteins are the structural engineers maintaining those skybridges, ensuring the buildings don't sway apart.

24:37.180 --> 24:37.480
Right.

24:37.480 --> 24:54.760
But when the ERK alarm wave sweeps through the city, the engineers drop their tools, let go of the bridges, run down the stairwells to the basements of the buildings, push their arms out into the soil below, and start physically dragging the entire city forward on treads.

24:55.340 --> 25:00.840
I mean, that is a highly evocative but fundamentally accurate way to visualize it.

25:01.020 --> 25:01.160
Yeah.

25:01.360 --> 25:06.060
Down at the basal protosomes, the ZO1 proteins are no longer acting as glue.

25:06.420 --> 25:07.160
What are they doing?

25:07.160 --> 25:20.700
They undergo a functional phase transition, they help the cell grip the underlying extracellular matrix, the biological soil as you called it, and they generate the immense traction force required to physically pull the cell body forward.

25:20.940 --> 25:23.600
They are actively driving invasive migration.

25:23.780 --> 25:26.200
But they can't stay down in the basement forever, right?

25:26.280 --> 25:27.280
Well, definitely not.

25:27.580 --> 25:32.000
If all the engineers are in the basement pulling the city, the skybridges at the top are left unattended.

25:32.340 --> 25:36.360
The tight junctions would fail and the tissue would rip itself apart as it tried to move.

25:36.360 --> 25:36.960
Exactly.

25:37.220 --> 25:40.980
Which is why the reversibility of this process is the most critical part.

25:41.100 --> 25:41.680
How does it reverse?

25:42.200 --> 25:47.660
Well, after the ERK wave passes over the cell, the biochemical environment returns to baseline.

25:48.060 --> 25:50.540
The phosphate tag is removed from the ZO1.

25:50.720 --> 25:51.920
So it changes shape again.

25:52.260 --> 25:52.500
Yes.

25:52.920 --> 26:03.080
It changes shape again, releases its grip from the protosomes at the bottom, and travels all the way back up to the apical junctions to aggressively rebuild the barrier.

26:03.080 --> 26:07.680
The engineers run back up the stairs and fix the bridges before the buildings collapse.

26:08.040 --> 26:08.280
Precisely.

26:08.540 --> 26:16.260
And because this whole sequence is triggered by a propagating wave, it means the entire tissue never loses its structural integrity all at once.

26:16.500 --> 26:17.540
That's the beauty of it.

26:17.620 --> 26:20.400
The wave induces a transient invasive window.

26:20.400 --> 26:26.960
Only a localized moving band of cells is in the basement migration phase at any given second.

26:27.180 --> 26:31.200
Because the cells behind the wave have already restored their top-level junctions.

26:31.360 --> 26:33.580
And the cells ahead of the wave haven't lost theirs yet.

26:33.960 --> 26:37.620
The tissue behaves as a continuously coupled dynamic medium.

26:37.620 --> 26:41.840
And fliction makes a very vital distinction here regarding the physics of this wave.

26:42.160 --> 26:43.980
Because this is not simple diffusion.

26:44.380 --> 26:44.620
Right.

26:45.000 --> 26:45.960
Let's talk about diffusion.

26:46.220 --> 26:47.300
We are all familiar with it.

26:47.680 --> 26:47.860
Yeah.

26:47.900 --> 26:53.280
If you drop a drop of blue ink into a glass of water, the ink molecules just randomly bounce around.

26:53.280 --> 26:58.980
And the color passively slowly spreads out until the whole glass is light blue.

26:59.320 --> 26:59.420
Right.

26:59.560 --> 27:03.620
Diffusion is driven by random thermal motion and concentration gradients.

27:03.760 --> 27:04.740
It dissipates energy.

27:04.740 --> 27:07.460
But the ERK wave doesn't act like ink and water.

27:07.740 --> 27:07.920
No.

27:08.200 --> 27:08.480
No.

27:09.060 --> 27:13.500
Fliction emphasizes that the ERK wave is a constraint-propagating front.

27:13.900 --> 27:15.500
A constraint-propagating front.

27:15.660 --> 27:15.900
Okay.

27:16.140 --> 27:16.620
Explain that.

27:16.660 --> 27:20.960
In simple diffusion, a signal just spreads and causes a quantitative change.

27:21.340 --> 27:26.440
But a constraint-propagating front actually alters the fundamental mathematical topology

27:26.440 --> 27:30.060
of what states are dynamically accessible to the biological system.

27:30.120 --> 27:31.020
So it changes the rules.

27:31.020 --> 27:34.720
It transiently changes the rules of the universe for that specific cell.

27:35.020 --> 27:36.860
I want to make sure I completely grasp this.

27:37.460 --> 27:42.440
What does it mean in physical terms to change the topology of accessible states for a cell?

27:42.740 --> 27:45.300
Well, think about the resting cell before the wave hits.

27:45.920 --> 27:49.960
The potosome-invasive state, the migrating state, is not just inactive.

27:50.420 --> 27:53.200
It is mathematically and biochemically inaccessible.

27:53.340 --> 27:53.920
It's forbidden.

27:54.280 --> 27:54.620
Exactly.

27:54.780 --> 27:55.280
It is forbidden.

27:55.580 --> 27:58.900
The cell is deeply locked into the junction maintenance regime.

27:59.460 --> 28:01.700
It cannot migrate, even if it wanted to.

28:01.700 --> 28:03.500
But the ERK wave...

28:03.500 --> 28:06.040
The ERK wave doesn't just bump into the cell and push it.

28:06.220 --> 28:06.380
Yeah.

28:06.480 --> 28:08.080
It opens a dynamical window.

28:08.400 --> 28:11.720
It unlocks a heavy door that was previously bolted shut.

28:12.200 --> 28:16.740
Allowing the Z01 to undergo that phase transition from the top of the cell to the bottom.

28:16.880 --> 28:17.120
Yes.

28:17.420 --> 28:20.460
And once the wave passes, that door bolts shut again.

28:20.840 --> 28:24.860
It is a moving wave of permission propagating through the physical space of the tissue.

28:24.860 --> 28:26.720
A traveling wave of permission.

28:26.960 --> 28:27.840
I love that phrasing.

28:28.520 --> 28:35.340
So now if we step back, we have these two glaring anomalies that traditional entity-centered biology just struggles to explain.

28:35.940 --> 28:43.560
We have the developmental trajectories of anxious brains dilating in variants spreading out over decades instead of shifting to a single broken state.

28:43.760 --> 28:47.760
And we have migrating skin tissues acting as coupled wave mediums.

28:47.760 --> 28:55.040
Using propagating fronts of permission to transiently reorganize their entire physical structure in a matter of hours.

28:55.200 --> 28:56.040
It's incredible.

28:56.220 --> 28:58.080
So how on earth do we unite them?

28:58.600 --> 29:02.520
What is the grand unifying theory that Fliction is proposing here?

29:02.700 --> 29:05.280
This is where we introduce the mathematical heart of the paper.

29:05.900 --> 29:11.460
This is the Relativistic Scalar Vector Plenum Framework, or RSVP.

29:11.460 --> 29:18.760
Okay, here is where it gets mathematically dense, but we are going to walk through it carefully, because the elegance of this is staggering.

29:19.180 --> 29:20.300
It really is beautiful math.

29:20.500 --> 29:30.740
Fliction claims that both the anxious brain and the migrating skin cells, time and space, decades and hours, are governed by the exact same field theoretic equations.

29:30.960 --> 29:31.340
They are.

29:31.820 --> 29:36.160
To understand this, we need to define the three interacting fields of the RSVP model.

29:36.160 --> 29:41.500
Let's define these three fields, because they are the foundation of this entirely new paradigm.

29:41.740 --> 29:47.040
And keep in mind, when we say field in this context, we don't necessarily mean a physical field.

29:47.100 --> 29:49.080
Like a magnetic field operating in a vacuum.

29:49.460 --> 29:50.160
Right, it's not that.

29:50.480 --> 29:57.800
We are talking about a distributed mathematical quantity defined over an abstract space of possibilities, an organizational state space.

29:57.880 --> 29:58.380
Okay, got it.

29:58.500 --> 30:01.540
So the first field is phi, represented by the Greek letter phi.

30:02.040 --> 30:03.940
Phi is the coherence field.

30:03.940 --> 30:10.140
Phi measures the degree of structural organization, or the local organizational density of the system.

30:10.240 --> 30:12.360
So it describes how locked in the system is.

30:12.600 --> 30:13.020
Exactly.

30:13.260 --> 30:18.940
It describes how deeply the biological system is locked into a specific, stable, coherent configuration.

30:19.240 --> 30:22.420
Okay, so in the cell migration study, what is phi?

30:22.840 --> 30:26.880
In the cells, high phi corresponds to the tight junctions being fully intact.

30:27.580 --> 30:33.140
Z01 is at the top, the tissue barrier is strong, and the cell is structurally committed to sitting still.

30:33.140 --> 30:35.040
And in the brain development study?

30:35.460 --> 30:39.280
High phi corresponds to a consolidated, stabilized neural architecture.

30:40.060 --> 30:46.700
The synaptic pathways are heavily myelinated, developmental plasticity is extremely low, and the brain structure is locked in.

30:47.080 --> 30:48.340
High phi is rigidity.

30:48.620 --> 30:49.220
It is structure.

30:49.480 --> 30:53.100
Okay, so phi is coherence, structural commitment, rigidity.

30:53.460 --> 30:57.340
The second field is V, represented mathematically as a vector.

30:57.680 --> 30:57.860
V.

30:58.140 --> 30:59.880
This is the vector transport field.

30:59.880 --> 31:04.840
The vector field V specifies the direction and the rate of active transporter propagation.

31:05.500 --> 31:07.780
It is the movement through the state space.

31:08.160 --> 31:10.900
So for the skin cells, V is quite literal.

31:11.100 --> 31:11.620
Very literal.

31:11.800 --> 31:18.320
It represents the physical ERK wave propagating across the two-dimensional surface of the epithelial tissue.

31:18.860 --> 31:20.420
It has a speed and a direction.

31:20.880 --> 31:22.620
And in the neural context of anxiety?

31:22.780 --> 31:25.220
It's a bit more abstract, but just as real.

31:25.640 --> 31:27.660
It represents the developmental drift velocity.

31:27.660 --> 31:33.700
It is the speed and direction the brain's architecture is moving through its developmental lifespan over the years.

31:33.860 --> 31:35.940
It is the flow of biological change.

31:36.140 --> 31:36.880
V is structure.

31:37.200 --> 31:38.080
V is flow.

31:38.220 --> 31:38.620
Exactly.

31:38.800 --> 31:49.020
And that brings us to the third field, which is really the conceptual breakthrough in Flixian's paper, S, which stands for entropic accessibility or the admissibility field.

31:49.080 --> 31:50.140
This is the star of the show.

31:50.340 --> 31:54.740
S measures the total volume of dynamically reachable configurations from the system's current state.

31:54.780 --> 31:55.400
So options.

31:55.400 --> 31:57.020
Yes, options.

31:57.700 --> 32:02.180
It is a rigorous mathematical measure of organizational plasticity or openness.

32:03.240 --> 32:09.100
Flixian calculates it operationally as roughly the negative logarithm of the local trajectory density.

32:09.740 --> 32:13.520
So S is approximately equal to negative logro.

32:13.520 --> 32:20.140
Okay, let's translate that math into a lived experience for the listener, because negative logro doesn't mean much to most of us.

32:20.260 --> 32:20.600
Fair enough.

32:21.360 --> 32:24.700
Think about when you first learned how to ride a bicycle as a kid.

32:24.900 --> 32:25.600
Oh, that's a good one.

32:25.860 --> 32:31.360
When you first get on the bike, your brain's S field for balance and motor control is huge.

